

<!-- ===== SOURCE: K1_Fever_Workup.md ===== -->


> [!warning] Sourcing
> Written from model knowledge, not retrieved from guidelines. Diagnostic reasoning, mechanism and discriminators are reliable at intern level. **Every temperature threshold, neutrophil cut-off, time target, dose and duration carries an `UNVERIFIED` marker naming what to check, or has been omitted with the omission stated in place.**
> **All empirical antimicrobial regimens are omitted** — obtain them from **eTG Antibiotic** and your health network's protocol, since local antibiograms drive the choice. Notifiable disease obligations must be checked against **SA Health**. Tropical and travel-related infection management should be confirmed with an infectious diseases service.
>
> Sepsis recognition and management is built in [[F0.3]] 0.7 and [[A1]] 0.2 and is cross-referenced rather than repeated.

---

## 0.1 Fever — Mechanism and the Approach

**D:** A **regulated** elevation of body temperature, produced by a rise in the hypothalamic thermoregulatory set point.

**A/P:** Exogenous pyrogens (microbial products) and tissue injury stimulate release of **endogenous pyrogens — interleukin-1, interleukin-6, tumour necrosis factor and interferons** → these act on the organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis → **prostaglandin E2** production → the hypothalamic set point rises → the body then **defends the new higher temperature** by vasoconstricting, shivering and seeking warmth.
**This is why a patient developing a fever feels cold and shivers** — they are below their new set point — and why they feel hot and sweat as the fever breaks and the set point falls.

> [!tip] Fever is not hyperthermia, and the distinction changes treatment
> **Fever** — set point raised, body defends it, **antipyretics work** because they inhibit prostaglandin synthesis and lower the set point.
> **Hyperthermia** — set point normal, heat production or retention exceeds dissipation. **Antipyretics do NOT work.** Heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, serotonin toxicity, thyroid storm.
> The full comparison is in [[A6]] 0.3. **Reaching for paracetamol in a hyperthermic patient wastes time in a condition where time matters.**

> [!danger] Absence of fever does not exclude infection
> **Blunted or absent febrile responses occur in: the elderly · neonates · the immunosuppressed and those on corticosteroids · uraemic and dialysis patients · those on regular paracetamol or NSAIDs · and in overwhelming sepsis.**
> **Hypothermia in a patient with infection is a marker of severity and a poor prognostic sign**, not reassurance.
> In an older person, **new confusion, a fall, reduced oral intake or functional decline may be the only sign of a serious infection.** Cross-refer [[GER1]] 0.1.

> [!warning] Fever patterns are over-taught and under-useful
> Tertian and quartan patterns in malaria, Pel-Ebstein fever in Hodgkin lymphoma, and relative bradycardia in typhoid are classical, examinable, and **unreliable in practice** — modified by antipyretics, timing of observations and treatment. **Do not exclude a diagnosis because the pattern does not fit.**
> **Relative bradycardia** (a heart rate lower than expected for the temperature) retains modest usefulness — described in typhoid, legionella, brucellosis, and **drug fever** — but is not diagnostic.

> [!tip] Not all fever is infection
> **· Malignancy** — lymphoma, leukaemia, renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma, atrial myxoma.
> **· Inflammatory** — connective tissue disease, vasculitis, **giant cell arteritis and polymyalgia rheumatica**, adult-onset Still disease, sarcoidosis, inflammatory bowel disease.
> **· Thromboembolic** — deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism cause low-grade fever.
> **· Drug fever** — see 0.6.
> **· Endocrine** — thyroid storm, adrenal crisis, phaeochromocytoma.
> **· Tissue necrosis and inflammation** — myocardial infarction, pancreatitis, resolving haematoma, gout, post-seizure.
> **· Transfusion reactions** — cross-refer [[A9]] 0.2.
> **· Factitious.**

**S/Smx:** Temperature, rigors, sweats, and the localising features of the source — which must be actively sought rather than waited for.

**Ix:** See 0.2.

### 0.1.1 Mx – Immediate
**Assess for sepsis first** — cross-refer [[F0.3]] 0.7. A febrile patient who is haemodynamically unwell gets cultures and antibiotics immediately; a febrile patient who is well gets a proper diagnostic assessment.

### 0.1.2 Mx – Definitive
Source-directed.

### 0.1.3 Mx – Chronic/long-term
Cause-specific.

---

## 0.2 Acute Undifferentiated Fever

> [!tip] The systematic source search — head to toe, because the source is usually findable
> **History:** onset and pattern · localising symptoms in every system · **exposures — travel (see 0.4), animals, occupational, sick contacts, food and water, recreational water, sexual history, injecting drug use** · immunisation status · recent hospitalisation, surgery or procedures · **indwelling devices** · antibiotics in the preceding weeks · immunosuppression · known colonisation with resistant organisms.
> **Examination — and these are the ones that get skipped:**
> **· Skin, in full** — rashes, wounds, **IV cannula sites**, pressure areas, perianal region, feet and between the toes.
> **· Mouth and teeth** — **dental abscess is a genuinely common occult source.**
> **· Ears, sinuses, throat.**
> **· Lymph nodes, all groups.**
> **· Heart — for a new murmur.** Cross-refer [[B5]] 0.2.
> **· Abdomen, including the flanks and a rectal examination where indicated.**
> **· Spine — percuss it.** **Discitis and epidural abscess present with fever and focal spinal tenderness, and are missed for weeks.**
> **· Joints** — a septic joint may be the source or a metastatic consequence.
> **· Calves** — DVT.
> **· Every line, drain, catheter and device.**

> [!danger] The commonly missed sources
> **Dental and sinus infection · prostatitis · epidural abscess and discitis · infective endocarditis · intra-abdominal or pelvic collection · perianal abscess · line and device infection · septic arthritis · pressure injury with underlying osteomyelitis · *Clostridioides difficile* · venous thromboembolism · and drug fever.**
> Several of these produce few localising signs and are found only because they were specifically looked for.

> [!warning] Do not reflexively start antibiotics in a stable patient with no source
> **In a haemodynamically stable, non-neutropenic patient without an identified source, obtaining cultures and observing is often better than empirical antibiotics.**
> Premature antibiotics **sterilise cultures and obscure the diagnosis** — this is precisely how endocarditis and fever of unknown origin become undiagnosable.
> **The exceptions where you treat immediately: sepsis or haemodynamic instability · neutropenia · asplenia · suspected meningitis · suspected necrotising infection · and the immunosuppressed.** In all of those, delay kills.

**Ix:** **Blood cultures — at least two sets from separate sites, before antibiotics** (*why:* the single most important microbiological test, and yield falls sharply once antibiotics are given; *what:* organism and sensitivities). **FBC with differential and blood film** (*why:* neutrophilia, neutropenia, lymphopenia, atypical lymphocytes, eosinophilia (parasites, drug reaction) and blasts all redirect the differential, and the film is frequently diagnostic; *what:* the pattern). CRP, and ESR where an inflammatory or malignant cause is considered (*why:* CRP tracks acute infection; **a very high ESR with a modest CRP points toward giant cell arteritis, myeloma or malignancy**; *what:* the discordance is itself informative). UEC, LFT, lactate (*why:* organ dysfunction and hypoperfusion; *what:* derangement). **Urinalysis and culture, interpreted with care** (*why:* urinary tract infection is common, but **asymptomatic bacteriuria is very common in the elderly and catheterised, and a positive urine does not prove the fever's source** — cross-refer [[F0.3]] 0.10; *what:* pyuria, organism, in clinical context). **CXR** (*why:* pneumonia may have no localising signs in the elderly; *what:* consolidation, effusion, mass). Site-directed cultures — sputum, wound, stool, CSF, joint aspirate, drain fluid (*why:* identifies the organism at the source; *what:* growth). **CT of the relevant region where a collection is suspected** (*why:* occult intra-abdominal, pelvic, spinal and thoracic collections require drainage and are invisible clinically; *what:* collection, abscess). Echocardiography where endocarditis is possible — **transoesophageal if transthoracic is negative and suspicion persists** (*why:* TTE has limited sensitivity, particularly for prosthetic valves; *what:* vegetation, abscess). Viral serology and PCR as directed (*why:* EBV, CMV, HIV, hepatitis and respiratory viruses; *what:* pathogen). **HIV testing** (*why:* it is a treatable diagnosis, it changes the entire differential, and it is under-offered — testing should be routine rather than reserved for those who "look at risk"; *what:* positive serology).

### 0.2.1 Mx – Immediate
Sepsis pathway if unwell. Cultures before antibiotics. Antipyretics and fluids for comfort.

### 0.2.2 Mx – Definitive
Source control and targeted antimicrobials.

### 0.2.3 Mx – Chronic/long-term
De-escalate on cultures and review duration daily.

---

## 0.3 Fever of Unknown Origin and Prolonged Fever

**D:** Fever above a defined threshold, persisting for a defined duration, **without a diagnosis despite an appropriate initial investigation.** `UNVERIFIED — the temperature threshold, duration and the definition of "appropriate initial investigation".` `CF-PAIR` **All three ARE in this file, at `08_09 ## Pyrexia of unknown origin (PUO) / Fever of unknown origin`, on the Petersdorf/Durack-Street criteria: fever >38.3°C on several occasions, >3 weeks, no cause after 3 days of inpatient evaluation or ≥3 outpatient visits. The marker stays — those figures are not verified against an Australian source here.**
Subcategories: **classic, nosocomial, neutropenic, and HIV-associated** — each with a different differential.

> [!tip] The four categories of cause, and roughly how they divide
> **1. Infection** — the largest single group. **Tuberculosis, particularly extrapulmonary and miliary** · **infective endocarditis** (especially culture-negative, or partially treated) · occult abscess — hepatic, subphrenic, pelvic, perinephric, dental · osteomyelitis and discitis · EBV and CMV · **Q fever, which is genuinely relevant in Australia — abattoir workers, farmers, veterinarians and those with livestock or kangaroo exposure, and there is a vaccine** · brucellosis · HIV.
> **2. Malignancy** — **lymphoma above all**, leukaemia, renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma, atrial myxoma, and metastatic disease.
> **3. Non-infectious inflammatory disease** — **giant cell arteritis and polymyalgia rheumatica, which are a leading cause in the over-50s and are treatable** · adult-onset Still disease (quotidian fever, evanescent salmon-pink rash, arthralgia, very high ferritin) · vasculitis · SLE · sarcoidosis · inflammatory bowel disease.
> **4. Miscellaneous** — **drug fever** · thromboembolism · thyroiditis · familial Mediterranean fever and other periodic fever syndromes · factitious fever.

> [!danger] The most productive step is to repeat the history and examination, not to order more tests
> **In a substantial proportion of cases the diagnosis is in a detail missed on the first pass** — an exposure not asked about, a rash that has come and gone, a drug started three weeks ago, a travel history that was taken as "no recent travel" when the patient meant no travel this month.
> **Take the history again, from the beginning, and examine the patient again, fully, including the skin, mouth, temporal arteries, spine and genitalia.** Repeat this as the illness evolves — new signs appear.
> **Also: stop every non-essential medication**, because drug fever is common, entirely reversible, and diagnosed only by cessation.

> [!warning] Resist empirical antibiotics and empirical steroids
> **Both obscure the diagnosis without curing it.** Empirical antibiotics sterilise cultures and partially treat endocarditis into a culture-negative state; empirical steroids suppress fever in lymphoma, tuberculosis and vasculitis alike, and can be catastrophic in undiagnosed infection.
> **The exception is suspected giant cell arteritis with visual symptoms**, where steroids are given immediately on suspicion because delay causes blindness. Cross-refer [[D1]] 0.4.

> [!tip] Targeted rather than shotgun investigation
> **Every test should be prompted by a clinical clue.** Undirected panels generate false positives that then require chasing.
> **PET-CT has become genuinely useful in FUO**, identifying occult infection, inflammation and malignancy and directing biopsy — it is one of the few "broad" investigations that earns its place. `UNVERIFIED — availability, funding and indications in Australia.`
> **Biopsy anything accessible and abnormal** — a lymph node, a skin lesion, a temporal artery, the bone marrow, or a liver lesion. **Histology diagnoses FUO more often than serology does.**

**Ix:** As per 0.2, plus: **repeated blood cultures, including prolonged incubation and specific request for fastidious organisms** (*why:* culture-negative endocarditis organisms — *Coxiella*, *Bartonella*, *Brucella*, HACEK — require specific culture conditions or serology and are missed on routine sets; *what:* fastidious organism). **Tuberculosis investigation — sputum, interferon-gamma release assay, imaging, and tissue where accessible** (*why:* extrapulmonary and miliary TB is a leading FUO cause and is missed because it does not present with a cough; *what:* AFB, granulomas, IGRA). **ESR, CRP, and temporal artery ultrasound or biopsy in the over-50s** (*why:* giant cell arteritis is common, treatable and sight-threatening; *what:* raised markers, arteritis). **Autoimmune serology — ANA, ENA, ANCA, rheumatoid factor, complement** (*why:* connective tissue disease and vasculitis; *what:* positive serology, interpreted in clinical context). **Ferritin** (*why:* a very high ferritin supports adult-onset Still disease and haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis; *what:* marked elevation). LDH and blood film (*why:* lymphoma and haematological malignancy; *what:* raised LDH, abnormal cells). Serology for **Q fever, *Bartonella*, *Brucella*, EBV, CMV, HIV, syphilis and hepatitis** (*why:* the treatable infective causes of prolonged fever; *what:* positive serology). **CT chest, abdomen and pelvis** (*why:* occult abscess, lymphadenopathy, malignancy; *what:* collection, nodes, mass). Echocardiography including transoesophageal (*why:* endocarditis; *what:* vegetation). **PET-CT** where the above is negative (*why:* localises occult inflammation and directs biopsy; *what:* focal uptake). **Bone marrow aspirate and trephine** (*why:* diagnoses haematological malignancy, disseminated infection including TB, and haemophagocytic syndromes; *what:* histology, culture). **Full medication review with cessation of non-essential drugs** (*why:* drug fever is diagnosed by resolution on stopping; *what:* defervescence within days).

### 0.3.1 Mx – Immediate
Rarely required unless the patient becomes unstable.

### 0.3.2 Mx – Definitive
Diagnosis-directed. **Infectious diseases involvement early**, rather than after months of undirected testing.

### 0.3.3 Mx – Chronic/long-term
A proportion of FUO resolves spontaneously without a diagnosis ever being made, and that is an acceptable outcome once serious causes have been excluded — **but it should be a considered conclusion with planned follow-up, not an abandonment.**

---

## 0.4 Fever in the Returning Traveller

> [!danger] Malaria until proven otherwise
> **Any fever in a person who has been in a malaria-endemic area — at any point in the preceding months, and regardless of prophylaxis — is malaria until excluded.**
> ***Plasmodium falciparum* can progress from first symptom to death within days**, and it is the diagnosis that must be made today, not tomorrow.
> **Exclusion requires repeated blood films — thick and thin — and/or a rapid diagnostic test.** A single negative film does not exclude malaria, because parasitaemia fluctuates; **three negative sets over consecutive days are conventionally required.** `UNVERIFIED — the number and timing of films, and the role of rapid diagnostic tests and PCR, from current Australian guidance.`
> **Prophylaxis does not exclude it** — adherence is imperfect and no regimen is fully protective.
> **Severe malaria** — impaired consciousness, seizures, acidosis, hypoglycaemia, renal failure, ARDS, high parasitaemia, jaundice, bleeding — is a medical emergency requiring parenteral treatment and ICU-level care.

> [!tip] Incubation period narrows the differential fast
> **· Short, under about 10 days** — **dengue, rickettsial infection, typhoid (early), influenza and other respiratory viruses, bacterial gastroenteritis, leptospirosis, meningococcal disease, malaria.**
> **· Intermediate, roughly 10–21 days** — **malaria, typhoid, leptospirosis, Q fever, brucellosis, viral haemorrhagic fevers.**
> **· Long, beyond 21 days** — **malaria (particularly *P. vivax* and *P. ovale*, which relapse from dormant liver hypnozoites months later), tuberculosis, viral hepatitis, amoebic liver abscess, leishmaniasis, HIV, schistosomiasis, filariasis.**
> **Take the exact itinerary with dates**, not just "Asia" — the countries, rural versus urban, and the activities.

> [!tip] The specific diagnoses worth knowing
> **· Dengue** — fever, severe myalgia and headache, retro-orbital pain, rash, **leucopenia and thrombocytopenia.** **Warning signs of severe dengue: abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, mucosal bleeding, lethargy, hepatomegaly and a rising haematocrit with a falling platelet count**, signalling plasma leakage. **Avoid NSAIDs and aspirin** because of bleeding risk. Management is supportive with careful fluid balance.
> **· Typhoid (enteric fever)** — insidious fever, headache, abdominal pain, **relative bradycardia**, rose spots, constipation more often than diarrhoea early. **Blood cultures are the key test.** Complications: intestinal perforation and haemorrhage in the third week.
> **· Rickettsial infections — scrub typhus, spotted fevers, murine typhus** — fever, headache, myalgia, rash, and **an ESCHAR at the bite site.** **Look for the eschar — in the axilla, groin, under the breast, behind the knee, in the scalp** — because it is easily missed and it makes the diagnosis. **Doxycycline is the treatment and is given empirically on suspicion**, since serology is retrospective.
> **· Leptospirosis** — freshwater exposure, flooding, rodents. Fever, myalgia (classically calf), **conjunctival suffusion**, and in severe (Weil) disease jaundice with renal failure and bleeding.
> **· Chikungunya and Zika** — with chikungunya causing prominent and prolonged arthralgia, and Zika carrying congenital risk.
> **· Amoebic liver abscess** — right upper quadrant pain and fever weeks to months after travel, often without diarrhoea.
> **· Schistosomiasis (Katayama fever)** — fever, urticaria and eosinophilia weeks after freshwater exposure.
> **· Acute HIV seroconversion** — fever, rash, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy, mouth ulcers. **Test for it; the antibody test may be negative early so an antigen/antibody assay or viral load is needed.**
> **· Viral haemorrhagic fever** — rare, but **if the epidemiology fits, isolate immediately and contact the infectious diseases service and public health before proceeding**, because laboratory handling and staff protection change.

> [!warning] Australian-acquired infections matter too — do not assume "travel" means overseas
> **· Melioidosis** — northern Australia, particularly during the wet season, and disproportionately affecting **people with diabetes, chronic kidney disease, harmful alcohol use, and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.** Presents as severe pneumonia, sepsis or abscesses. **Requires specific prolonged antimicrobial therapy** and is missed if not considered. Cross-refer [[F0.5]] 0.4.
> **· Q fever** — abattoir, farm, veterinary and livestock exposure. **A vaccine exists and is recommended for at-risk occupations**, which is a preventive point worth knowing.
> **· Murray Valley encephalitis and Japanese encephalitis** — mosquito-borne flaviviruses with recent Australian activity; consider in encephalitis after inland or riverine exposure.
> **· Ross River and Barmah Forest virus** — fever, rash and prominent polyarthralgia, common and often prolonged.
> **· Leptospirosis** — in farm and flood exposure.

**Ix:** **Thick and thin blood films, repeated, plus a rapid diagnostic test** (*why:* malaria is the diagnosis that kills fastest and a single negative film does not exclude it; *what:* parasites, species, parasitaemia percentage). FBC with film (*why:* **thrombocytopenia is near-universal in malaria and dengue and is a useful early clue; eosinophilia suggests helminth infection**; *what:* platelets, eosinophils, atypical lymphocytes). **Blood cultures** (*why:* typhoid, melioidosis, bacteraemia; *what:* organism). LFT, UEC, glucose, lactate, coagulation (*why:* severity assessment in malaria, dengue and leptospirosis, and **hypoglycaemia is a feature of severe malaria**; *what:* organ dysfunction). **Dengue NS1 antigen, IgM and PCR** (*why:* stage-dependent — NS1 and PCR early, serology later; *what:* positive result). Serology and PCR for **rickettsia, leptospirosis, Q fever, arboviruses** (*why:* often retrospective, so treatment is empirical; *what:* seroconversion). **HIV antigen/antibody test** (*why:* seroconversion illness, and a treatable diagnosis; *what:* positive). Stool microscopy, culture and parasite examination (*why:* enteric pathogens and parasites; *what:* organism, ova and cysts). CXR and targeted imaging (*why:* pneumonia, melioidosis, abscess; *what:* consolidation, collection). **Notification to public health where required** (*why:* legal obligation and outbreak control; *what:* the notifiable list — `UNVERIFIED — check the SA Health notifiable conditions list`).

### 0.4.1 Mx – Immediate
**Exclude malaria urgently.** Isolate where a high-consequence pathogen is possible. Treat severe malaria as an emergency with parenteral therapy. Empirical doxycycline where rickettsial infection is suspected. Supportive care with careful fluid management in dengue.

### 0.4.2 Mx – Definitive
Pathogen-specific therapy with infectious diseases involvement. **Radical cure with primaquine for *P. vivax* and *P. ovale* to eradicate liver hypnozoites — requiring G6PD testing first**, because primaquine causes haemolysis in G6PD deficiency.

### 0.4.3 Mx – Chronic/long-term
**Pre-travel advice for future trips** — vaccination, malaria prophylaxis, bite avoidance, food and water precautions. Notification and contact tracing where required.

---

## 0.5 Fever in the Immunocompromised, and Febrile Neutropenia

> [!danger] Febrile neutropenia is a medical emergency
> **Antibiotics are given within a defined short interval of presentation — before the workup is complete, before the source is known, and before the neutrophil count returns if the history makes neutropenia likely.**
> **Mortality rises with every hour of delay.** In practice this means: **recognise it at triage, take cultures immediately, and give the antibiotic.** `UNVERIFIED — the fever and neutrophil thresholds defining febrile neutropenia, the door-to-antibiotic time target, and the empirical regimen, all from eTG Antibiotic and local protocol.` `CF-PAIR` **Two of the three ARE in this file, at `NEW_Infectious_Diseases ## Fever in the Immunocompromised`: ≥38.5°C once or ≥38.0°C sustained, ANC <0.5 ×10⁹/L (or <1.0 and predicted to fall), and antibiotics within 60 minutes. **THE EMPIRICAL REGIMEN IS NOT** — that account independently declines to state it, and gives the reason (local resistance patterns, hospital protocol, egress-blocked). So the regimen half of this marker stands.**

> [!danger] There may be no localising signs, because the signs require neutrophils
> **Pus, erythema, consolidation on chest radiograph, and peritonism are all products of the neutrophilic inflammatory response — which the patient does not have.**
> A neutropenic patient with pneumonia may have a normal chest radiograph. One with a perianal abscess may have only pain. **The absence of findings is not reassurance; it is expected.**
> **Examine carefully but gently — mouth, skin, perianal region, all line sites, and the chest.** **Avoid digital rectal examination and rectal thermometers in neutropenic patients**, because of the risk of translocation and bacteraemia.

> [!tip] Risk stratification and escalation
> The **MASCC score** identifies a low-risk group who may be suitable for oral therapy and early discharge — but this is a decision made with haematology or oncology, not independently. `UNVERIFIED — the score and its threshold.`
> **Escalation to add cover for resistant Gram-positive organisms** (line infection, skin or soft tissue infection, haemodynamic instability, known colonisation) and **empirical antifungal therapy for persistent fever despite broad-spectrum antibacterials** are standard steps. **Persistent fever after several days on appropriate antibacterials should raise invasive fungal infection**, particularly with prolonged neutropenia.

> [!warning] Other forms of immunocompromise, and what each predisposes to
> **· Corticosteroids and other immunosuppressants** — bacterial infection, **Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia** (with prophylaxis indicated above certain steroid doses and durations), reactivation of latent TB and herpesviruses, and **fungal infection.**
> **· Biologic agents** — **TNF inhibitors cause reactivation of latent tuberculosis, which is why screening before starting them is mandatory.** **Rituximab causes hepatitis B reactivation** (screen and prophylax) and predisposes to PJP and progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy. **Eculizumab predisposes to meningococcal disease** and mandates vaccination.
> **· Solid organ transplant** — the differential follows a **post-transplant timeline**: nosocomial and surgical infections in the first month; **opportunistic infections (CMV, PJP, fungal, reactivation) from one to six months**; and community-acquired plus late opportunistic infections thereafter.
> **· HIV** — the differential is **stratified by CD4 count**, with PJP, cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcal meningitis, CMV retinitis and disseminated *Mycobacterium avium* complex appearing at progressively lower counts. **A febrile patient of unknown HIV status should be tested.**
> **· Asplenia and hyposplenism** — **encapsulated organisms**, and the risk of **overwhelming post-splenectomy infection**, which progresses from well to dead within hours. **These patients need immediate antibiotics with any fever**, plus vaccination, prophylaxis and a medical alert. Cross-refer [[C1]] 0.5.
> **· Neutropenia from other causes** — drugs (**clozapine, carbimazole, methotrexate, co-trimoxazole**), marrow failure, and haematological malignancy.

> [!warning] Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome
> As immune function recovers — after starting antiretroviral therapy, after neutrophil recovery, or after withdrawing immunosuppression — **a previously subclinical infection can become inflamed and appear to worsen dramatically.**
> **This can be mistaken for treatment failure**, and the management is different: continue treating the infection, and manage the inflammation, sometimes with steroids.

**Ix:** **Blood cultures from a peripheral vein AND from each lumen of any central line** (*why:* paired cultures with differential time to positivity identify a line as the source, which determines whether the line must be removed; *what:* organism, time to positivity). **FBC with differential urgently** (*why:* establishes neutropenia and its depth; *what:* absolute neutrophil count). UEC, LFT, CRP, lactate (*why:* organ dysfunction and severity; *what:* derangement). **CXR — recognising that it may be normal in neutropenia** (*why:* consolidation requires neutrophils to form; *what:* infiltrate if present). **CT chest with high-resolution sequences where fever persists** (*why:* far more sensitive than radiography for invasive fungal disease and early pneumonia in neutropenia; *what:* nodules, halo sign, infiltrates). Urine, stool, sputum and site-directed cultures (*why:* source identification; *what:* organism). **Fungal markers — galactomannan, beta-D-glucan — where invasive fungal infection is suspected** (*why:* supports diagnosis before culture; *what:* positive marker). Viral PCR — CMV, respiratory viruses, EBV (*why:* reactivation and community viruses in the immunosuppressed; *what:* viral load). **HIV testing** (*why:* changes the entire differential; *what:* positive). CD4 count and viral load in known HIV (*why:* stratifies the opportunistic infection differential; *what:* CD4 level). Careful examination of mouth, skin, perineum and line sites (*why:* the source is often visible and there is little else to find; *what:* mucositis, cellulitis, exit site infection).

### 0.5.1 Mx – Immediate
**Cultures then immediate empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics.** Resuscitate. Involve haematology, oncology or infectious diseases. **In asplenia, the same urgency applies to any fever.**

### 0.5.2 Mx – Definitive
De-escalate on culture. Escalate cover or add antifungals per protocol for persistent fever. Remove infected lines. Growth factor support where indicated.

### 0.5.3 Mx – Chronic/long-term
**Prophylaxis, vaccination and patient education** — every immunocompromised or asplenic patient should know that fever means presenting immediately, and should carry documentation. **Screening for latent TB and hepatitis B before starting biologics.** Cross-refer [[K3]].

---

## 0.6 Post-Operative and Drug Fever

> [!tip] The post-operative timeline narrows the differential
> **· Within 24 hours** — usually the **inflammatory response to surgery itself**, which is common and benign. Also consider **malignant hyperthermia** (cross-refer [[A6]] 0.3), transfusion reaction, and a pre-existing infection.
> **· Days 1–3** — **respiratory**: pneumonia and aspiration. **Note that atelectasis as a cause of fever is poorly supported by evidence despite being taught for decades** — it is associated with early post-operative fever rather than causing it, and attributing fever to atelectasis can delay finding the real source.
> **· Days 3–5** — **urinary tract infection (especially catheterised) and intravenous line phlebitis or infection.**
> **· Days 5–7** — **surgical site infection, anastomotic leak, and intra-abdominal collection.** Cross-refer [[C7]] 0.5.
> **· Beyond a week** — **venous thromboembolism, deep collection or abscess, *C. difficile*, and drug fever.**
> The traditional mnemonic is the **five Ws — Wind, Water, Wound, Walking, Wonder drugs** — useful as a prompt, with the atelectasis caveat above.

> [!danger] The post-operative patient who is not progressing
> **Persistent or new fever from around day five, with ileus that will not settle, unexplained tachycardia, rising inflammatory markers, or simply not recovering as expected, means a collection or an anastomotic leak until proven otherwise.** **New post-operative atrial fibrillation is a recognised early flag.**
> Cross-refer [[A1]] 0.5 and [[C7]] 0.5.

> [!warning] Drug fever
> **A diagnosis of exclusion, but a common one, and completely reversible.**
> Features: fever that may be high, in a patient who **looks better than their temperature suggests**; sometimes **relative bradycardia, eosinophilia, or a rash** — but frequently none of these. It may begin days to weeks after the drug was started.
> **Common culprits: beta-lactams, sulfonamides including co-trimoxazole, anticonvulsants (phenytoin, carbamazepine), allopurinol, heparin, antituberculous drugs, and antipsychotics.**
> **Diagnosis is by cessation — fever typically resolves within a few days of stopping the offending agent.** `UNVERIFIED — the expected time to defervescence.`
> **Do not forget the more serious drug-related febrile syndromes: DRESS (with eosinophilia and organ involvement — cross-refer [[A5]] 0.6), neuroleptic malignant syndrome and serotonin toxicity (cross-refer [[A6]] 0.3), and transfusion reactions (cross-refer [[A9]] 0.2).**

> [!tip] The other non-infective causes in an inpatient
> **· Venous thromboembolism.**
> **· Resolving haematoma and tissue necrosis.**
> **· Alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal.**
> **· Thyroid storm and adrenal crisis.**
> **· Gout and pseudogout**, which can produce a strikingly high fever with a raised white cell count and be mistaken for sepsis.
> **· Pancreatitis, myocardial infarction, and post-seizure.**

**Ix:** Systematic re-examination including **wound, drains, lines, calves and perineum** (*why:* most post-operative sources are visible or palpable and are found by looking; *what:* wound infection, line site, collection, DVT). Blood cultures, urine culture, wound swab where clinically infected, sputum culture (*why:* organism identification; *what:* growth). **CT of the operative region where a collection or leak is suspected** (*why:* the definitive investigation, and clinical signs are unreliable in the post-operative abdomen; *what:* collection, free gas, extraluminal contrast). CXR (*why:* pneumonia, aspiration, effusion; *what:* consolidation). ***C. difficile* testing where diarrhoea and recent antibiotics** (*why:* common and requires isolation; *what:* toxin, PCR). Doppler ultrasound or CTPA where thromboembolism is suspected (*why:* fever from VTE is easily attributed to infection; *what:* thrombus). **FBC with differential** (*why:* eosinophilia suggests drug fever or DRESS; *what:* eosinophils). **Full medication chart review with a timeline** (*why:* drug fever is diagnosed only by considering it and stopping the drug; *what:* temporal relationship).

### 0.6.1 Mx – Immediate
Assess for sepsis. Cultures. Examine the wound and every device. Treat identified infection.

### 0.6.2 Mx – Definitive
Source control — drainage, line removal, return to theatre for a leak. Cessation of the suspected drug.

### 0.6.3 Mx – Chronic/long-term
**Document a drug fever clearly as an adverse reaction** so the agent is not re-prescribed. Antimicrobial stewardship review.

---

> [!note] Cross-references
> Sepsis definitions, phenotypes and management → [[F0.3]] 0.7 and [[A1]] 0.2 · Fever versus hyperthermia and the drug-induced hyperthermic syndromes → [[A6]] 0.3 · Meningococcal sepsis → [[F0.3]] 0.9 · Urosepsis and asymptomatic bacteriuria → [[F0.3]] 0.10 · Infective endocarditis and new murmur → [[B5]] 0.2 · Severe CAP and melioidosis → [[F0.5]] 0.4 · Meningitis and encephalitis → [[D1]] 0.5 · Transfusion reactions → [[A9]] 0.2 · Post-operative collections and anastomotic leak → [[C7]] 0.5 and [[A1]] 0.5 · Severe cutaneous adverse reactions and DRESS → [[A5]] 0.6 · Post-splenectomy prophylaxis → [[C1]] 0.5 · Giant cell arteritis → [[D1]] 0.4 · Atypical presentation in the elderly → [[GER1]] 0.1 · Skin and soft tissue infection → [[K2]] · Exposure, prophylaxis and immunodeficiency → [[K3]] · Neutropenia and haematological causes → [[J1]]


